[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs. A
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m write to a pipe whose reader has gone away therefore returns `EPIPE`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cio::Out::raw_write` discards, and the program runs to a normal exit. The C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program keeps the default disposition, so the same write kills it with signal
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 13. Exit status and the amount of output flushed both differed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is only observable when the consumer of stdout exits early, which is why it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m survived every test that captured output in full — it is invisible to a harness
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that reads the whole stream.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
