[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the wrap explicitly and is profile-independent.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Regression tests** — `remove_shape_index_int_min` covers the input;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `release_and_test_profile_binaries_agree` runs the same input through both the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m release binary and the binary cargo builds for the test profile and requires
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m identical stdout, stderr and exit status. Reverting to `shape_idx - 1` makes the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m latter fail.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Non-mismatch worth recording: `%p` cannot be compared byte for byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scene_list_shapes`, `add_shape_to_scene` and `compare_shapes` print heap
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m addresses with `printf("%p")`. Those are not reproducible **even between two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs of the C program**, because the heap base is randomised:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ ./c_src/build/driver <<< $'9\n0\n0\n12\n' | grep ptr
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Shape 1: Tree (ptr: 0xa8bb2b0)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ ./c_src/build/driver <<< $'9\n0\n0\n12\n' | grep ptr
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Shape 1: Tree (ptr: 0x1d4bb2b0)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No implementation can match those bytes. What *is* observable, and what the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program's logic depends on, is the identity relation the addresses encode: which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printed pointers are equal and which differ (`shape_equals` is pointer equality,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `Comparison of pointers: %d` prints the result). The harness therefore
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m replaces each distinct `0x<hex>` run with a token numbered by first appearance,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so two outputs compare equal only if they agree byte for byte everywhere else
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **and** print the same pattern of equal/distinct pointers in the same positions.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The harness additionally asserts that every printed pointer has glibc's `%p`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m shape (`0x` followed by lowercase hex) in both programs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviour deliberately preserved rather than "fixed"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These all looked like defects and were kept because the C does them:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`while (getchar() != '\n');` never terminates at end of file.** `getchar()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   keeps returning `EOF`, which never equals `'\n'`, so any numeric prompt reached
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   exactly at EOF spins forever. `cio::In::eat_until_newline` spins the same way.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Covered by `eof_inside_the_scanf_drain_loop_spins_in_both`, which asserts that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   *both* programs are still running when the time limit expires and that neither
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   has flushed anything.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` crosses newlines, `fgets` does not.** Numeric prompts skip blank
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   lines and consume a value from any later line; scene names and filenames stop
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   at the first newline and are truncated at 63 and 255 bytes respectively, with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the remainder left in stdin to be re-read as the next menu choice.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf`/`sscanf` `%d` saturates then truncates.** glibc converts via the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `strtol` family (saturating at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`) and stores the result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   into an `int`. `99999999999999999999` becomes `-1`, `-99999999999999999999`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   becomes `0`, `4294967296` becomes `0`. `cio::finish_int` reproduces this.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **A sign with no digits still consumes the sign.** `-x` at a numeric prompt is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   a matching failure, but `-` has already been read, so the drain loop only has
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `x\n` left.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`fopen` on a directory succeeds.** `scene_load("somedir")` opens it, the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   first `fgets` fails, and the function returns `NULL` **without** printing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   anything — no "could not open" message. `scene_load` mirrors this by treating a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   read error as the failing first `fgets`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Over-capacity loads still succeed.** A saved file claiming more than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `MAX_SHAPES_IN_SCENE` shapes writes `Error: Scene is full` to stderr once per
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   extra shape and then reports the scene as loaded.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Out-of-range shape types in a file are silently skipped**, not rejected, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the loaded scene can have fewer shapes than the file's count.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`strcspn(buf, "\n")` stops at a NUL**, so a NUL byte in a scene name or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   filename truncates it there.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Input classes covered
